ation during steady-state visual-evoked responses. <source>J Neurosci</source> <volume>23</volume>: <fpage>11621</fpage>&#x02013;11627, <year>2003</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14684864</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r20"><label>Pastor et al. 2006</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Pastor</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name>, Thut G, Pascual-Leone A. Modulation of steady-state auditory evoked potentials by cerebellar rTMS. <source>Exp Brain Res</source> <volume>175</volume>: <fpage>702</fpage>&#x02013;709, <year>2006</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16826412</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r21"><label>Penhune et al. 1998</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Penhune</surname><given-names>VB</given-names></name>, Zattore RJ, Evans AC. Cerebellar contributions to motor timing: a PET study of auditory and visual rhythm reproduction. <source>J Cogn Neurosci</source> <volume>10</volume>: <fpage>752</fpage>&#x02013;765, <year>1998</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9831742</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r22"><label>Penny et al. 2004</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Penny</surname><given-names>WD</given-names></name>, Stephan KE, Mechelli A, Friston KJ. Comparing dynamic causal models. <source>Neuroimage</source> <volume>22</volume>: <fpage>1157</fpage>&#x02013;1172, <year>2004</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15219588</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r23"><label>Ramnani et al. 2000</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Ramnani</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name>, Toni I, Josephs O, Ashburner J, Passingham RE. Learning- and expectation-related changes in the human brain during motor learning. <source>J Neurophysiol</source> <volume>84</volume>: <fpage>3026</fpage>&#x02013;3035, <year>2000</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11110829</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r24"><label>Ram&#x000f3;n 1911</label><citation citation-type="other"><name><surname>Ram&#x000f3;n y Cajal</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name> <source>Histologie du Syst&#x000e8;me Nerveux de l'Homme et des Vert&#x000e9;br&#x000e9;s.</source> Paris: A. Maloine, et al. <year>1911</year> &#x0005b;original French edition&#x0005d;.</citation></ref><ref id="r25"><label>Ribary et al. 1991</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Ribary</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name>, Ioannides AA, Singh KD, Hasson R, Bolton JP, Lado F, Mogilner A, Llin&#x000e1;s R. Magnetic field tomography of coherent thalamocortical 40-Hz oscillations in humans. <source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source> <volume>88</volume>: <fpage>11037</fpage>&#x02013;11041, <year>1991</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1763020</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r26"><label>Rohrbaugh et al. 1990</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Rohrbaugh</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name>, Varner JL, Paige SR, Eckardt MJ, Ellingson RJ. Event-related perturbations in an electrophysiological measure of auditory sensitivity: effects of probability, intensity and repeated sessions. <source>Int J Psychophysiol</source> <volume>10</volume>: <fpage>17</fpage>&#x02013;32, <year>1990</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2269644</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r27"><label>Ross et al. 2005</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name>, Herdman AT, Pantev C. Stimulus induced desynchronization of human auditory 40-Hz steady-state responses. <source>J Neurophysiol</source> <volume>94</volume>: <fpage>4082</fpage>&#x02013;4093, <year>2005</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16107530</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r28"><label>Ross and Pantev 2004</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name>, Pantev C. Auditory steady-state responses reveal amplitude modulation gap detection thresholds. <source>J Acoust Soc Am</source> <volume>115</volume>: <fpage>2193</fpage>&#x02013;2206, <year>2004</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15139631</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r29"><label>Ross et al. 2002</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name>, Picton TW, Pantev C. Temporal integration in the human auditory cortex as represented by the development of the steady-state magnetic field. <source>Hear Res</source> <volume>165</volume>: <fpage>68</fpage>&#x02013;84, <year>2002</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12031517</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r30"><label>Sato et al. 1992</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Sato</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name>, Miura A, Fushiki H, Kawasaki T. Short-term modulation of cerebellar Purkinje cell activity after spontaneous climbing fiber input. <source>J Neurophysiol</source> <volume>68</volume>: <fpage>2051</fpage>&#x02013;2062, <year>1992</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1491256</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r31"><label>Schmahmann et al. 1999</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Schmahmann</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name>, Doyon J, McDonald D, Holmes C, Lavoie K, Hurwitz AS, Kabani N, Toga A, Evans A, Petrides M. Three-dimensional MRI atlas of the human cerebellum in proportional stereotaxic space. <source>Neuroimage</source> <volume>10</volume>: <fpage>233</fpage>&#x02013;260, <year>1999</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10458940</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r32"><label>Schmahmann and Pandya 1991</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Schmahmann</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name>, Pandya DN. Projections to the basis pontis from the superior temporal sulcus and superior temporal region in the rhesus monkey. <source>J Comp Neurol</source> <volume>308</volume>: <fpage>224</fpage>&#x02013;248, <year>1991</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1716269</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r33"><label>Schmahmann and Pandya 1997</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Schmahmann</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name>, Pandya DN. The cerebrocerebellar system. <source>Int Rev Neurobiol</source> <volume>41</volume>: <fpage>31</fpage>&#x02013;60, <year>1997</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9378595</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r34"><label>Steriade et al. 1991</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Steriade</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name>, Dossi RC, Par&#x000e9; D, Oakson G. Fast oscillations (20&#x02013;40 Hz) in thalamocortical systems and their potentiation by mesopontine cholinergic nuclei in the cat. <source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source> <volume>88</volume>: <fpage>4396</fpage>&#x02013;4400, <year>1991</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2034679</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r35"><label>Tesche and Karhu 2000</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Tesche</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name>, Karhu JJ. Anticipatory cerebellar responses during somatosensory omission in man. <source>Hum Brain Mapp</source> <volume>9</volume>: <fpage>119</fpage>&#x02013;142, <year>2000</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10739364</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r36"><label>Tiitinen et al. 1993</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Tiitinen</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name>, Sinkkonen J, Reinikainen K, Alho K, Lavikainen J, Naatanen R. Selective attention enhances the auditory 40-Hz transient response in humans. <source>Nature</source> <volume>364</volume>: <fpage>59</fpage>&#x02013;60, <year>1993</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8316297</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r37"><label>Van Horn and Sherman 2007</label><citation citation-type="journal"><name><surname>Van Horn</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name>, Sherman SM. Fewer driver synapses in higher order than in first order thalamic relays. <source>Neuroscience</source> <volume>146</volume>: <fpage>463</fpage>&#x02013;470, <year>2007</year>.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17320295</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r38"><citation citation-type="other">Wellcome Department of Cognitive Neurology. SPM'99. <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm/">http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm/</ext-link>. Accessed November 30, <year>2001</year>.</citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>The online version of this article contains supplemental data.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="f1"><label>FIG. 1.</label><caption><p><italic>A</italic>, <italic>B</italic>, and <italic>C</italic>: graphic describing the 3 dynamic causal models (DCMs), constituting 3 areas: auditory superior temporal gyrus/superior temporal sulcus (STG/STS), the medial geniculate body of the thalamus, and Crus II in the cortical cerebellar hemisphere. The specification of the modulatory (<italic>B</italic> or bilinear) connections varied according to 3 different DCMs.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="z9k0100890900001"></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="f2"><label>FIG. 2.</label><caption><p><italic>Left</italic>: depicts the frequency spectrum of echo planar imaging gradient waveforms. <italic>Right</italic>: a detailed look at the low-frequency spectrum, showing the relation with the auditory frequencies used in the experiment.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="z9k0100890900002"></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="f3"><label>FIG. 3.</label><caption><p>Schematic of the experimental design; showing the temporal sequence of click trains during the scanner noise, intermingled with white noise bursts and ensuing motor responses.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="z9k0100890900003"></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="f4"><label>FIG. 4.</label><caption><p>I: axial, coronal, and sagittal sections of the canonical space showing activations during the auditory 40-Hz epochs, relative to the remaining 2 frequencies. The statistical parametric mapping (SPM) results are shown as a slice through the cerebellum Crus II (Hemispheric clusters x, y, z: &#x02212;40, &#x02212;78, &#x02212;30 and 38, &#x02212;82, &#x02212;32). SPM threshold <italic>p</italic><sub>svc</sub> corrected &#x0003d; 0.05. II: axial, coronal, and sagittal sections of the canonical T1 MR template depicted as boundaries of the group of 10 individual activations in auditory STG/STS (<italic>A</italic>), the medial geniculate body (<italic>B</italic>), and cortical cerebellar hemisphere (<italic>C</italic>), used for DCM volumes of interest.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="z9k0100890900004"></graphic></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="t1"><label>TABLE 1.</label><caption><p>Log-Bayes factors for each subject</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center" valign="bottom">Subject</th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center" valign="bottom">ln <italic>B</italic><sub>12</sub></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center" valign="bottom">ln <italic>B</italic><sub>13</sub></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center" valign="bottom">ln <italic>B</italic><sub>23</sub></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top">S01</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">50.499584700729571</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">101.0504919158240</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">50.550907215094412</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top">S02</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">50.285374241003979</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">100.8363654921294</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">50.550991251125424</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top">S03</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="char" char="." valign="top">50.453069426240461</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="cha